--------------------------------------------------------------------
Third-party components
--------------------------------------------------------------------

This project stands on work done by others. Where possible we embed or
depend on their code rather than reimplementing it — that is a
deliberate design choice, not a shortcut.

adblock-rust — Mozilla Public License 2.0
  https://github.com/brave/adblock-rust
  Brave's content blocking engine. Embedded to provide ad, tracker and
  cosmetic filtering. We do not implement our own blocking engine.

Tauri — Apache License 2.0 / MIT
  https://github.com/tauri-apps/tauri
  Application shell across desktop and mobile, running on each
  platform's own webview.

TensorFlow.js — Apache License 2.0
  https://github.com/tensorflow/tfjs
  On-device inference runtime (tfjs-core, tfjs-converter, and the CPU
  and WebGL backends) powering the gaze protection module.

TensorFlow Lite (org.tensorflow:tensorflow-lite, -gpu, -gpu-api 2.16.1)
— Apache License 2.0
  https://github.com/tensorflow/tensorflow
  Native on-device inference runtime on Android: the same three
  models (BlazeFace, faceres, MoveNet) converted to .tflite and run
  through the GPU delegate at fp32, bridged to the page over a
  WebMessagePort. The WebGL runtime above stays as the fallback.

Qualcomm QNN LiteRT delegate (com.qualcomm.qti:qnn-litert-delegate,
qnn-runtime) — EVALUATED 2026-09-03, NOT USED, NOT VENDORED
  https://repo1.maven.org/maven2/com/qualcomm/qti/qnn-litert-delegate/
  https://docs.qualcomm.com/bundle/publicresource/topics/80-63442-50/tflite_delegate.html

  Considered for the performance batch's NPU auto-try (NativeInfer.kt's
  CONFIG path). The Maven artifact's POM licenses it under the
  "Qualcomm AI Hub Model License" (Qualcomm Innovation Center, Inc.),
  fetched from
  https://softwarecenter.qualcomm.com/api/download/software/licenses/ai_model_hub/v1/LICENSE.pdf.

  Section 2 (LICENSE RESTRICTIONS), 2.c lists applications the licensee
  agrees NOT to use the Software for or in connection with, including
  verbatim:

    "biometric and biometrics-based systems, including categorization
    of persons based on sensitive characteristics"

  and:

    "scraping of facial images (from the internet or otherwise)"

  This engine's entire job is exactly the first of those: BlazeFace
  detects faces in video frames and faceres classifies each one by
  gender (and age), which is categorization of persons by a sensitive
  characteristic. That is not an edge case the app happens to touch —
  it is what person-gate.mjs / detector.js / this whole native tensor
  runner exist to do. Accelerating that pipeline on a QNN NPU delegate
  would be using the Software "for or in connection with" a forbidden
  application, independent of the separate question (Section 1, which
  otherwise appears to permit distributing the Software in object code
  "as incorporated in Your software application") of whether the
  compiled .aar could be shipped inside the APK at all.

  So: NOT added to build.gradle.kts, NOT linked, NOT distributed.

  The NPU path that IS used is Android's own Neural Networks API through
  TensorFlow Lite's NnApiDelegate (org.tensorflow.lite.nnapi, part of
  the Apache-2.0 tensorflow-lite artifact already listed above). NNAPI
  routes to whatever accelerator driver the phone's vendor shipped in
  its firmware; this project distributes no vendor code and accepts no
  vendor licence for it. NativeInfer.kt's loadModel() tries NNAPI
  behind the NATIVE_NPU dial and keeps it only where it measures faster
  than the GPU/CPU candidate and agrees with it on the outputs.

BlazeFace model — Apache License 2.0
  https://github.com/tensorflow/tfjs-models (Google / MediaPipe)
  Face-presence detection model, embedded (base64) in the gaze
  module's bundle so no network fetch is ever needed.

nsfwjs — MIT License
  https://github.com/infinitered/nsfwjs
  On-device NSFW classification; the MobileNetV2 model files shipped
  in the nsfwjs package are embedded the same way as BlazeFace.

dsojevic/profanity-list — MIT License
  https://github.com/dsojevic/profanity-list
  Seed keyword list for the protection engine's text signals. A
  snapshot lives at app/gaze/vendor/profanity-en.json; only the
  sexual/shock-tagged entries are embedded in the gaze bundle.

obscenity — MIT License
  https://github.com/jo3-l/obscenity
  Text-matching engine (evasion normalization: leetspeak, confusables,
  repeated characters) used by the protection engine's text signals.

vladmandic/human and human-models — MIT License
  https://github.com/vladmandic/human
  The gaze module's BlazeFace box decode + anchor generation are
  adapted from Human's implementation (see app/gaze/src/detector.js),
  and the gender classifier (faceres.json — HSE-FaceRes, originally
  from HSE-asavchenko/HSE_FaceRec_tf) is the converted model
  distributed in vladmandic/human-models, embedded (base64) like the
  other models; its reader in detector.js is adapted from Human's
  faceres.ts. (Earlier gender models from the same repo — SSR-Net until
  2026-08-23, Oarriaga mini-Xception until 2026-08-24 — were replaced
  after calibration showed them unusable on real thumbnails.)

EasyList / EasyPrivacy — dual-licensed GPLv3 / CC BY-SA 3.0
  https://easylist.to/
  Consumed as upstream filter data by the embedded engine. Snapshots
  live under rules/vendor/ and keep their own licences (they are NOT
  covered by rules/'s CC0 dedication).

uBlock Origin filter lists — GNU GPLv3
  https://github.com/uBlockOrigin/uAssets
  Same treatment: vendored data snapshots under rules/vendor/, their
  licence intact.
  Owner ruling 2026-09-05: these stay in every build, store builds
  included. They are filter DATA the engine reads at runtime, not code
  compiled into the binary -- the same relationship Brave and AdGuard
  have with the same files -- and every YouTube ad-strip rule the app
  relies on lives here (68 rules against 1 in EasyList). uBlock's
  scriptlet CODE is a different matter and is never used; see below.

Brave adblock resources (resources.json) — Mozilla Public License 2.0
  https://github.com/brave/adblock-resources
  Scriptlet/redirect resource definitions consumed by the engine.

